LEARNING OBJECTIVES

Compare dense retrieval with keyword-based retrieval and understand hybrid search.

CORE CONCEPTS

Dense retrieval uses embeddings to find semantically related passages. It is useful when a question expresses an idea differently from the original text.

BM25 is a keyword-based ranking method. It considers term frequency, document frequency, and document length. It can perform well when a query contains exact terms, identifiers, technical names, or specific phrases.

Hybrid retrieval combines dense and keyword-based signals. A simple implementation can normalize scores and combine them using a weighted sum. Another approach is Reciprocal Rank Fusion, which combines rankings rather than relying directly on raw scores.

A weighted approach can be expressed as:

combined_score = alpha * dense_score + (1 - alpha) * bm25_score

The weight alpha controls the relative contribution of dense retrieval. The scores must be made compatible before combining them because raw scores from different methods may not be directly comparable.

Retrieval quality should be tested on a representative question set rather than assumed from the algorithm alone.

APPLICATION TO STUDENT SECOND BRAIN

Dense retrieval can find conceptually similar course material, while BM25 can find exact terms such as MAX_STEPS, no_answer, or MCP. Hybrid retrieval can combine these strengths.

KEY TAKEAWAY

Dense, BM25, and hybrid retrieval have different strengths. Compare them using the same test set and inspect the retrieved sources.